WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.708
YUFENG GUO: Is your
model training slower

00:00:01.708 --> 00:00:02.880
than you'd like?

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:05.850
Perhaps your GPU isn't getting
the kind of utilization

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:06.900
you think it should.

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:08.400
Then you've come
to the right place,

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:11.580
because we're going to level up
your understanding of storage

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:14.970
options for your machine
learning and data science

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:16.198
workloads.

00:00:16.198 --> 00:00:19.544
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:29.350
Welcome to AI Adventures, where
we explore the art, science,

00:00:29.350 --> 00:00:31.043
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:31.043 --> 00:00:31.960
My name is Yufeng Guo.

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:33.627
And on this episode,
we're going to take

00:00:33.627 --> 00:00:37.152
a look at various storage
options on Google Cloud.

00:00:37.152 --> 00:00:39.360
When thinking about running
machine learning and data

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:41.700
science workloads in
the cloud, one factor

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:44.220
that is often
overlooked is storage.

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:46.050
It's easy to take
storage for granted.

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:48.870
In our day to day, storage
just happens on the hard drives

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:50.200
of our phones and laptops.

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:52.770
But in the cloud, things
are a little bit different.

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:55.350
Depending on your use case
and your collaborators,

00:00:55.350 --> 00:00:58.443
your data storage requirements
may have vast differences.

00:00:58.443 --> 00:01:01.110
So today, we're going to look at
some different types of storage

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:03.810
options on Google Cloud and
see what kinds of trade-offs

00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:05.670
are being made so
that you can pick

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:07.950
the right tool for your task.

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:10.470
Oftentimes when we're doing
tasks on Google Cloud,

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:14.550
the default is to just stick
files on Google Cloud Storage,

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:17.040
which I'll refer to as GCS.

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:19.210
You make a new bucket,
stick some files in it,

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:19.990
and you're done.

00:01:19.990 --> 00:01:23.400
Since GCS can be accessed
over the network via REST API

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:26.770
calls often wrapped in
a command line or SDK,

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:29.440
it's become very convenient
for a quick and easy storage

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:31.440
solution, when you need
to drop a few files here

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:34.140
and there to relay
some bits around.

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:36.360
The data gets replicated
across multiple regions

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:37.590
if you need to.

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:40.350
You only pay for the exact size
of the files you're storing.

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:42.750
And sharing is relatively easy.

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:46.410
This makes GCS especially
useful for systems that operate

00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:48.580
in a serverless environment.

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:52.470
But sometimes, you need the
performance of block storage.

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:54.198
Enter the persistent disk.

00:01:54.198 --> 00:01:55.740
Yes, indeed, we're
moving all the way

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:58.410
down the stack to be closer
to the compute resource

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:01.000
and closer to the raw hardware.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:03.940
OK, virtualized raw hardware.

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:05.880
These disks offer
high-performance

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:06.780
reads and writes.

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:09.240
And your code sees it
as a local file system,

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:11.640
which can be very handy
for migrating existing

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:13.680
workflows into the cloud.

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:15.390
Virtual machines in
a Google data center

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:18.180
are scattered across
many physical machines.

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:19.830
And they're all
connected together

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:22.440
via the fast local network.

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:25.350
That applies not only to
compute, but to storage

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:26.380
as well.

00:02:26.380 --> 00:02:29.430
So what we see as a
single persistent disk

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:32.550
is actually many slices of
different hard drives scattered

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:34.510
all over the data center.

00:02:34.510 --> 00:02:36.480
This also means
that you can easily

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:38.820
connect multiple
virtual machines

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:41.310
to the same persistent
disk, as well

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:45.930
as connect multiple persistent
disks to one virtual machine.

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:47.970
All of these slices
can read and write

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:51.660
in parallel, which is why
larger persistent disks will

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:55.210
have faster Io
performance up to a point.

00:02:55.210 --> 00:02:57.780
We can see that in this
graph, read speeds--

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:01.740
they don't top out until after
you have a 10-terabyte disk,

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:03.840
while sustained
throughput maxes out

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:08.680
around 400 megabytes per second
at the 4-terabyte disk size

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:10.020
mark.

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:14.100
Persistent disks have
three tiers of choices--

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:17.940
standard, SSD, and local SSD.

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:20.850
Standard persistent disks
are your good old spinning

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:23.730
hard drive and are the
backbone of most data

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:25.890
storage systems out there.

00:03:25.890 --> 00:03:29.010
SSDs up the read and
write performance.

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:31.350
But it is still
distributed across the data

00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:35.500
center in the same way as
standard persistent disks.

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:40.050
Now, local SSD is co-located
with your compute resource.

00:03:40.050 --> 00:03:44.880
And it comes in exact
increments of 375 gigabytes.

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:46.920
So you should really
only use it if you're

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:49.710
sure that you're going to need
that high performance read

00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:50.940
and write.

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:54.300
Additionally,
standard and SSD disks

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:57.120
both offer zonal and
regional options.

00:03:57.120 --> 00:03:59.100
With regional
persistent disks, you

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:01.530
can failover to a
second zone in the event

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:04.830
that your primary zone has an
outage, enabling you to have

00:04:04.830 --> 00:04:07.440
high availability services.

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:09.030
In machine learning
use cases, you

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:11.610
can take advantage of the
high I/O and throughput

00:04:11.610 --> 00:04:14.450
performance of persistent
disks by loading large data

00:04:14.450 --> 00:04:16.860
sets onto a persistent
disk and then

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:20.589
attaching them in read only
mode to many, many instances.

00:04:20.589 --> 00:04:23.550
This will enable your team to
work with very large data sets

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:26.490
without duplicating the
data, all while everyone

00:04:26.490 --> 00:04:29.910
gets to feel like the data is
right there on their own VM

00:04:29.910 --> 00:04:31.750
instance.

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:33.850
Note that for workloads
that primarily

00:04:33.850 --> 00:04:36.360
involve small random reads--

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:39.490
4 kilobytes to 16 kilobytes--
the limiting factor

00:04:39.490 --> 00:04:43.570
is random Input/output
Operations Per Second, or IOPS.

00:04:43.570 --> 00:04:46.270
But for workloads
that involve primarily

00:04:46.270 --> 00:04:49.300
sequential or
large random reads,

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:52.600
like 256 kilobytes
up to 1 megabyte,

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:55.220
the limiting factor
is throughput.

00:04:55.220 --> 00:04:58.300
This means that you should set
the block size of your disk

00:04:58.300 --> 00:05:02.090
to match the type of
use case you anticipate.

00:05:02.090 --> 00:05:04.030
So which storage
option will you choose?

00:05:04.030 --> 00:05:05.710
Well, that'll depend
on your use case.

00:05:05.710 --> 00:05:09.010
From the scaling flexibility
of GCS to the high performance

00:05:09.010 --> 00:05:11.440
of persistent disks,
there's a storage option

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:14.530
suitable for every type of
machine learning workload.

